---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Prose from '../../components/Prose.astro';
import { totalSoundCount } from '../../data/sounds';
import { article, breadcrumbs, organization } from '../../lib/seo';

const headline = 'How every LidShutter sound is made in code';
const summary = 'No recordings, no audio files: how LidShutter builds its sounds from scratch.';
---

<Base
  title={headline}
  description={summary}
  home={false}
  schema={[
    organization,
    article(headline, summary, '/blog/sounds-made-in-code'),
    breadcrumbs([
      { name: 'LidShutter', path: '/' },
      { name: 'Blog', path: '/blog' },
      { name: headline, path: '/blog/sounds-made-in-code' },
    ]),
  ]}
>
  <Prose eyebrow="Blog" title="How every LidShutter sound is made in code">
    <p>
      LidShutter has {totalSoundCount} sounds, and none of them is a recording. There are no audio files in the app at all.
      Every sound is built from scratch, in code, the moment it plays.
    </p>

    <h2>Building a shutter from tiny clicks</h2>
    <p>
      The Rolling Shutter sound starts as a stream of short metallic clicks that speed up and rise in pitch, like a shutter
      gathering speed as it rolls. Under them, a soft tone glides upward. At the top comes a deep “ka-chunk”, and then a
      little marimba “ding”.
    </p>

    <h2>A small toolbox of building blocks</h2>
    <p>Most sounds are made from the same handful of pieces, combined in different ways:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Glides</strong>: a tone that slides between two pitches, for whooshes, chirps and pops.</li>
      <li><strong>Marimba notes</strong>: a warm tone with a bright overtone, for dings and fanfares.</li>
      <li><strong>Bubbles</strong>: a quick upward sweep that sounds like a water drop.</li>
      <li><strong>Filtered noise</strong>: shaped hiss for wind, waves, rain and thunder.</li>
      <li><strong>Wobbles</strong>: a tone with vibrato, for springs and rubber ducks.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why make sounds this way?</h2>
    <p>
      Sounds made in code are tiny, have nothing to license, and are created in a few milliseconds. They also let
      LidShutter do things a recording can’t, like making a sound louder past 100% with a soft limiter so it stays clean.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can hear every one of them on the <a href="/#roll">LidShutter homepage</a>. The website plays them with the same
      recipes the app uses.
    </p>
  </Prose>
</Base>
